    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Public Services",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S1293\n\nSPONSOR: PARKER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act directing the department of public service to prepare a written\nreport on the affordability of utility services\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of the legislation is to conduct a comprehensive study of\nthe effect of the COVID-19 state of emergency, on utilities and consum-\ners.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 provides the legislative findings.\n\nSection 2 lays out the definitions applicable to this act.\n\nSection 2 also requires utilities to submit data regarding arrears and\naffordability to the PSC for the PSC to produce an initial report and an\nupdated report on the effect of the COVID-19 state of emergency on\narrears and affordability of utility services in New York State.\n\nSection 2 also allows utilities and municipalities to petition the PSC\nif they believe the data required to be submitted to the PSC by this act\ncannot be reasonably obtained without an increase in customer utility\nbills.\n\nSection 2 also requires the PSC to publish the reports required pursuant\nto Subsection 2 of the act to its website simultaneously with the\nsubmission of each report to the governor and legislature.\n\nSection 3 is the severability clause.\n\nSection 4 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nBy the end of year one of the COVID-19 pandemic (e.g., February 2020 to\nMarch 2021), more than 1.4 million households owed energy utilities in\nexcess of $1.5 billion- a number has increased further today. Little is\nknown concerning debt to non-energy utilities because the State of New\nYork has not collected such data prior to this legislation. This massive\narrears crisis has undoubtedly placed pressure on essential public\nservices, including utility services, and could hold back economic\nrecovery in large areas of the State.  Public utilities, including muni-\ncipally-owned utilities, were required to continue operation during the\npandemic irrespective of their customers' inability to pay their bills\nin part or full. All utilities, including municipal and public authority\nutilities, were required to continue providing service to consumers\nnotwithstanding nonpayment, and were forbidden to terminate service or\nengage in normal collections activity. The passage of a utility morato-\nrium in New York was essential, as proper sanitation was, and is, a\ncrucial weapon in the fight against the spread of the COVID19 virus.\nHowever, there remains an existing financial impact for both customers\nand utility companies that must be studied to provide evidence necessary\n\nfor an adequate legislative response to assist New York's economy to\ngrow its way out of the COVID-19 Recession.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021-22: S5451C - Vetoed by Governor - Vetoed Memo.73\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
